//! Alternative implementations benchmarked against `exp`.
//!
//! The formulation choices of `fixed::exp` that were ambiguous enough to be implemented twice
//! (`docs/DESIGN.md` rule 9); the winners live in the library, the losers stay here with their
//! benches (`bench_exp::alt_*`):
//!
//! | choice | library | here |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | `2^g` evaluation | 1 024-entry table of `2^(k/16)` + degree 6 on `[0, 1/16)` | 64-entry table
//! of `2^k` + degree 12 on `[0, 1)` (`exp2_single_poly`) |
//! | `exp` reduction | `exp2` of the wide product `x * log2(e)` (56 bits) | Cody-Waite by `ln(2) /
//! 16` + `e^r` polynomial (`exp_cody_waite`); `exp2(x * LOG2_E)` at 32 bits (`exp_naive`, fast but
//! `2^15` ULP off at `e^11`) |
//! | exponent search of `log2` | unrolled binary search, 6 comparisons (`normalize_tree`) | 6
//! conditional shifts (`normalize_clz`); 3 halvings + a 256-entry `msb` table
//! (`normalize_msb_table`) |
//! | `log2(m)` | 32 segments x degree 4 in the exact mantissa | `atanh` series in `s = (m - 1) / (m
//! + 1)`, one division (`log2_atanh`) |
//!
//! The polynomials and the tables below are generated by `scripts/gen_exp.py`, like those of
//! the library. Unlike the library these variants use the public API only (no
//! `fixed::internal`), so their final rescale is a floor where the library rounds.
use fixed::exp::ExpTrait;
use fixed::wide::{W1, W2, WideAdd, WideLift, WideMul, WideNarrow, WideSub, wide_from, wide_mul};
use fixed::{Fixed, ONE, ZERO};

